{
  "abstract": "Quadratic jet determinant estimates require a small common row space for many original columns, node radii and derivative selections. We project complete physical columns in the existing Jacobi space and use two forms of the classical Meixner expansion. Forward differences give a positive shifted-charge estimate with a binomial spectral factor. An exact resolvent identity then retains cancellation between shifted differences. Its rational generating function need not have a finite entire Fock norm. We instead prove a local Gaussian disk comparison for the original finite coefficient prefix and combine it with a smaller coefficient circle on the remaining beta interval. At the unchanged source $R=907199985$ and retained rank $k=5288976$ per charge, a directed certificate proves squared residual below $e^{-R/125}$ for every integer $381023994\\le d\\le417311994$, all three prescribed radius ranges and their original column prefixes. Paying $36288000$ charges in the original half-open band and $39172200$ nodes gives residual trace below $\\exp(-0.00799996154048047568R)$. This improves the uniform specialization of the published Gramian bound at that rank. A separate difference specialization gives the stronger single-charge depth $0.00949997802829957$. We supply complete proofs, exact coefficient and derivative controls, and reproducible directed CPU calculations. The special-function identities are classical; the contribution is their compatible finite-source application. These are single-rank estimates: the earlier Gramian theorem retains the cumulative profile and complete determinant consumer. No additional global determinant credit, discriminant endpoint, census or classification is claimed. Imported arithmetic consequences retain their original qualifications.",
